{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1969-fourM-halfwave",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Radiant power flow and absorptance in thin films,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 423-436 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.000423 - Fig. 23 caption, p. 434: air (HL)^7 H 1.845L M1 0.5D1 HH 0.5D1 M2 0.5D2 HH 0.5D2 M2 0.5D1 HH 0.5D1 M1 1.845L (HL)^9 quartz",
  "Notes": "Four-M UV bandpass filter with half-wave H layers in the centre of each spacer to remove the 3000 A leak\nSpecification: Four-M filter with a half-wave H centred in each spacer.\nReported: p. 435: 'The peak transmittance of 0.45 is still attained, and an attenuation of five optical density units is attained on either side of the passband' (Fig. 23, computed with Hunter's dispersive Al data; log T 1e-6 to 1, peak ~0.45 near 0.25 um, ~1e-5 at 0.22 um and below 1e-5 beyond ~0.3 um). Check computed here (nondispersive Al): T = 0.4496, R = 0.001 at 253.6 nm; FWHM ~3.7 nm.\nNotes: Normal incidence assumed (no angle given). The notation is read left to right from the incident medium (air) to the substrate (quartz, n = 1.47), so layer 1 is next to air. All dielectric layers are quarter-wave optical thickness (QWOT) at lambda0 = 2536 A = 253.6 nm with constant indices; the Al film is a physical thickness with the nondispersive n - jk = 0.20 - j2.80 at 2536 A (Macleod sign convention; k = +2.80 here). The paper's Fig. 23 curve is dispersive (Hunter, J. Phys. 25, 154 (1964)); this record uses the nondispersive constant, so off-band values will differ from Fig. 23 while the peak should agree. HH is entered as one 2-QWOT layer of the 1.55 material. Expanded: (HL)^7 (14), H, 1.845L, M1, 0.5D1, 2H, 0.5D1, M2, 0.5D2, 2H, 0.5D2, M2, 0.5D1, 2H, 0.5D1, M1, 1.845L, (HL)^9 (18): 48 layers (51 if each HH counts as two).\nLayers from Fig. 23 caption, p. 434: air (HL)^7 H 1.845L M1 0.5D1 HH 0.5D1 M2 0.5D2 HH 0.5D2 M2 0.5D1 HH 0.5D1 M1 1.845L (HL)^9 quartz; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours T 0.450, R 0.001 at 253.6 nm; paper T 0.45 with R = 0 at 253.6 nm.",
  "Wavelength": [
    253.6
  ],
